<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/advanced/reinforcement-learning">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Reinforcement learning (RL) trains agents by <strong>reward, not instruction</strong>: try
    things, score outcomes, adjust behaviour toward more reward. It is the learning paradigm of
    games, robotics and control — and the hardest of the four paradigms, because the feedback is
    delayed, sparse and the agent shapes its own training data by acting.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the RL loop and its three classic headaches" widget="equationStep" config={rlCfg} />

  <h2>Where RL shines and struggles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Simulated environments are its natural habitat:</strong> games (Atari, Go, StarCraft, Dota 2 — OpenAI Five) allow unlimited cheap trial and error. Real robots break; simulators don't.</li>
    <li><strong>RLHF brought RL to language:</strong> human preferences become the reward; the LLM is the agent. The alignment lesson covers what that buys and costs.</li>
    <li><strong>Sim-to-real gap:</strong> policies trained in simulation meet friction, weight and chaos in reality. Domain randomisation (vary the sim wildly) and real-world fine-tuning are the bridges.</li>
    <li><strong>Reward hacking is the field's cautionary tale:</strong> a Coast-Sim boat spun in circles hitting targets; a Tetris agent paused the game forever to avoid losing. Rewards are proxies — and proxies get gamed (the alignment lesson expands).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sutton & Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction (free); Silver et al., Nature (2016) AlphaGo; Mnih et al., Nature (2015) DQN.</p>
</TopicPage>
